{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Baltimore Orioles arrived as one of baseball's most pleasant surprises, a young and hungry club that refused to follow the script of a rebuilding team. April was rough — a 7-14 start suggested another lost season was in store — but even in those early stumbles, flashes of brilliance emerged. Cedric Mullins announced himself with a grand slam, and the roster began to show the charisma that would define the summer ahead.\n\nMay changed everything. The Orioles caught fire, rattling off a 14-16 month that masked a genuine surge in the second half of the month. Rougned Odor emerged as an unlikely catalyst, and Austin Hays delivered clutch hit after clutch hit in a remarkable late-May stretch. Anthony Santander added his own late-game heroics, and suddenly Baltimore felt like a team worth watching. The arrival of Adley Rutschman — baseball's most anticipated prospect — provided the spark that transformed a losing club into a legitimate contender.\n\nJuly was the heart of the Orioles' story. Despite some brutal late-inning losses, particularly Jorge López's struggles out of the bullpen including a brutal pair of home runs surrendered in Minnesota, the team posted a 16-9 record — their best month of the season. Clutch hits from Rutschman, Mullins, Odor, Ryan Mountcastle, and Ramón Urías carried the day, and for a stretch in midsummer Baltimore sat comfortably in wild card position, daring the rest of the AL to take them seriously.\n\nAugust maintained the momentum at 17-10, but September proved to be the Orioles' undoing. A 13-15 September, punctuated by late bullpen collapses against Houston and Toronto, cost them dearly. The team finished 83-79, four wins short of a wild card berth. A final-weekend home run by prospect Terrin Vavra — a bittersweet sign of things to come — ended a season that fell just short but planted the seeds for a dynasty in the making.",
